Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A contract theory must be justified, not merely proved":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One objection that Rawls addresses is that of the idea that contract theories are fundamentally unacceptable because they represent mere agreement, and it seems that mere agreement cannot constitute a moral theory.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common critique of contract theories.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Rawls argues that since he began with commonly accepted premises, a logical deduction to some final conclusion is more than merely a proof of that final conclusion, it is in fact a justification for that final conclusion. It “proves” something more than mere agreement, and establishes a sort of metaphysical weight behind the result of that final conclusions, in his case, justice as fairness.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>This defense against the arguments seems rather weak in my opinion. It seems like a weak Moorean shift.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The goal of the city is to foster virtue\">The goal of the city is to foster virtue</a><ul><li>Aristotle would disagree with Contractarianism, and this note would be one of his critiques.</li></ul></li></ul>","Absurdity manifests through cleavages which are impossible to overcome":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Absurd most directly manifests through cleavages between two diametrically opposed concepts, which both must, to the human mind, exist in some capacity.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the nature of the Absurd.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Some examples of such cleavages include:</p><ul><li>The mind &amp; nature vs. Unity.</li><li>The eternal vs. The finite.</li><li>“Concern” vs. The ultimate vanity of one’s actions.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","All humans are innately good":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that when we are first born, we are naturally good, or at least that there are good elements within us. According to Rousseau, it is rather society which corrupts us and makes us capable of doing evil things.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important for many different philosophies, for example, Rousseau’s ideas. This also relates to the thought of some Eastern philosophers, for instance, Mencius.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Mill claims that humans innately want to act in ways which are beneficial to others (in the absence of external reasons).</p><h5>Confucianism &amp; Neo-Confucianism</h5><p>Mencius also argued for this, saying that people are born naturally with good elements and neutral elements.</p><ul><li>He developed the idea of the four cardinal/constant virtues, which originate as the “four beginnings”:<ul><li>The feeling of commiseration is the beginning of benevolence or human-heartedness.</li><li>The feeling of shame and dislike is the beginning of righteousness.</li><li>The feeling of modesty and yielding is the beginning of propriety.</li><li>The feeling of right ant and wrong is the beginning of wisdom. </li></ul></li></ul><p>Neo-Confucianist theory, especially from the <strong>School of Principle</strong>, argues that one is born with the Supreme Ultimate, and it is through a careful “investigation of things to extend knowledge” (格物致知) that we are able to find the Supreme Ultimate within ourselves and thus become a sage.</p><ul><li>This process involves investigating the things (<em>qi</em>) first, and then, though that process, one is then able to understand that which exists in the eternal world, the <em>li</em>.</li><li>Additionally, this enlightenment requires “attentiveness of mind” (用敬), in order to accomplish, since otherwise this process becomes merely intellectual.</li></ul><p>In the <strong>School of Mind</strong>, it is said that the individual possess <em>liangzhi</em> (良知), which is a universally shared, innate sense of morality. The goal of the individual then, is to act uninterfered by selfish desire, which my obscure the intentions of the <em>liangzhi</em>.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Society corrupted innately good humans\">Society corrupted innately good humans</a><ul><li>This involves the belief that humans were at least originally born good.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Humans are born as blank slates, gaining knowledge a posteriori\">Humans are born as blank slates, gaining knowledge a posteriori</a><ul><li>It seems that if humans are born as blank slates, they must be either naturally predisposed to be neutral or good.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Moral truths arise internally and naturally\">Moral truths arise internally and naturally</a><ul><li>This is the view of the School of Mind.</li></ul></li></ul>","Control of the Eurasian rimland dictates control of the world":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Nicholas Spykman’s Rimland Thesis holds that control of the Rimland affords its controller the ability to control the entire Eurasian continent, which would then allow the Eurasian power to control the entire world.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The Rimland Thesis informed the policy of containment practice by the US during the Cold War, a crucial aspect of American foreign policy in the latter half of the 20th century.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p> The Rimland Thesis finds several flaws in its predecessor:</p><ol><li>Most economic and military power is concentrated in the Rimland, rather than the Heartland.</li><li>The geographic features that surround the heartland can act also as a hedge against Heartland expansion outwards. </li><li>The ability for the Rimland to be connected via an inter-oceanic highway makes it just as effective moving large amounts of men, materials, and ideas across its mass as an industrialized Heartland.</li><li>There has never really been a simple land-sea power opposition.</li></ol><p>Therefore, Spykman focuses far more on inter-Rimland conflict, and its implications. Through his analysis, he concludes that the US must act as a mediator of the balance of power for Rimland states, especially in the “core areas,” such as Western Europe and East Asia. By establishing regional international organizations, nations would be able to maintain this balance of power amongst themselves, which they would do actively since the regional nature of such an organization ensures that they have a stake. The US simply acts as an outside mediator that ensure no state or coalition in the Rimland gets too powerful. This theory would come to influence the theory of containment during the Cold War, which would have immense impacts on not only US history but World History.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Kennan’s containment policy sought to prevent the expansion of Soviet power in the Rimland\">Kennan’s containment policy sought to prevent the expansion of Soviet power in the Rimland</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take\">Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Control of the Eurasian heartland dictates control of the world\">Control of the Eurasian heartland dictates control of the world</a></li></ul>","Free will is the ability to act against immediate desire, which makes it an instrument of character formation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Freedom consists in the capacity to do what is immediately unpleasant or undesirable, which means that its exercise is what builds character rather than merely expressing it.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This account makes free will continuous with virtue, since repeated exercise of the capacity is precisely how a disposition is formed.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle holds the view in this form. It differs from the Platonic account in that the object of freedom is not the good as such but the capacity to override present inclination, whatever its object.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>The mechanism by which the exercise of this capacity becomes a settled state.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires\">Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires</a><ul><li>The same capacity, described as a state rather than as a freedom.</li></ul></li></ul>","Great Britain narrowly avoided civil war in 1832 as a result of the Reform Bill":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Great Britain was narrowly able to prevent a revolution in 1832 as a result of the Reform Bill which drastically updated the medieval (literally) electoral laws in place at the time, which greatly disenfranchised the masses.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>An example of when reform prevented revolution.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Following 1848, government shifted from repression to reform to avoid further revolutions\">Following 1848, government shifted from repression to reform to avoid further revolutions</a><ul><li>The other governments of Continental Europe did not reform in the same way that Britain did, and thus faced revolutions, though the revolutions were crushed.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fall of the Roman Republic could be partly attributed to the fact that the Senate failed to adequately represent the Plebeians\">The fall of the Roman Republic could be partly attributed to the fact that the Senate failed to adequately represent the Plebeians</a><ul><li>The failure of the Roman Senate to reform was what doomed them.</li></ul></li></ul>","Great art is surprising":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A work is good to the extent that its parts are not predictable from one another, so that each element withholds something the audience could have supplied itself.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This gives a criterion for aesthetic quality that can be applied locally, at the level of the sentence or the brushstroke, rather than only to the work as a whole.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In prose the failure case is the adjective already implied by its neighbour, as in describing a sunset as interesting and incredible, where the second word adds nothing. The positive techniques are defamiliarisation, in which the commonplace is described as an outsider would encounter it, and syntactic misdirection, in which a construction sets an expectation that the next clause withdraws.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great art contains layers and internal echoes that bind it together\">Great art contains layers and internal echoes that bind it together</a><ul><li>The second criterion, which constrains this one: surprise alone would license mere randomness.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We find something funny because it is a logical non-sequitur\">We find something funny because it is a logical non-sequitur</a><ul><li>The same mechanism operating in humour.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suggestiveness, not articulateness, is the ideal of Chinese art\">Suggestiveness, not articulateness, is the ideal of Chinese art</a><ul><li>A tradition that makes withholding the central principle rather than a technique.</li></ul></li></ul>","In empires, the north is typically where the political control is based, and the south is typically where the economic power is":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is often the case in large land-based empires that the political control originated from the North, while the economics prosperity originates from the South.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a general pattern that can be observed in many states and can explain their formation/the things that they do.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>China</li><li>US</li><li>Russia</li><li>India</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take\">Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take</a><ul><li>This is an example of this principle in practice.</li></ul></li></ul>","Inefficient taxes are a means of keeping the power of the government in check":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Inefficiency in taxes are a means by which the people resist against the encroachment of state power. The people want to be taxed less, so they put up less resistance to taxes that are more inefficient, and since they put up resistance to taxes that are more efficient, the government chooses not to tax those, and instead tax the easier-to-tax but more inefficient options.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the persistence of inefficient taxes in the economy.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The high tax rates of the 1950s were not actually paid, and resulted in rent-seeking behavior\">The high tax rates of the 1950s were not actually paid, and resulted in rent-seeking behavior</a><ul><li>This is an example of when people resist taxes.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The land value tax (LVT) is the best form of taxation\">The land value tax (LVT) is the best form of taxation</a><ul><li>The reason that the LVT has not been implemented could be that it is too efficient, and thus by implementing it, the government would gain too much power.</li></ul></li></ul>","Justice and injustice can manifest in-whole, discretely, or through sum-threshold criteria":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>(In)justice can manifest through various forms, including</p><ul><li>in its total form at once, </li><li>in a discrete form in a particular rule in an institution or in an institution among many institutions, or </li><li>apparently non-existently, though in sum, produces a visible effect.</li></ul><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the various forms that justice could take.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice has lexical priority over other societal concerns\">Justice has lexical priority over other societal concerns</a><ul><li>When considering this, then, we have to also consider what form justice takes.</li></ul></li></ul>","Long-term bond yields are driven by expectations of inflation, not worries about government debt":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Long-term bond yields are driven primarily by expectations of long-term inflation, not worries about the government defaulting on its debt.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This argues against the thesis that long-term bond yields are the result of people’s fears of government default.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Debt levels do matter somewhat for countries with credible central banks, though the main driver of long-term bond yields is still inflation.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"TIPS yields are not a good proxy for real interest rates\">TIPS yields are not a good proxy for real interest rates</a><ul><li>Even if this note is true, it is not immediately applicable in the case of TIPS.</li></ul></li></ul>","Most profits from AI have gone to producers of hardware":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The primary limiting factor (the most limited complementary factor) for AI is hardware, since the supply is quite constricted. This also means that most of the gains from AI does to hardware companies.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the apparent non-effect of AI on the economy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There is pretty strong evidence, mainly in the double digit growth of Taiwan’s GDP in Q1 and Q2 of 2026, that most of the financial gains of AI have gone to the chip producers.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capital&#x27;s share of income will rise in the future due to AI\">Capital's share of income will rise in the future due to AI</a><ul><li>This is supported by this claim, since physical capital (and extremely valuable capital at that) is required in order for the development of AI.</li></ul></li></ul>","People value present goods more than future goods":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea of time preference in economics. This note explores various theories of time preference.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Time preference is one of the most important concepts in economics, since it explains things like interest rates and people consumption behavior.</p><h4>Theories</h4><p>Some theories to explain the existence of time preference includes:</p><ol><li>(Böhm-Bawerk) Current goods are able to provide both for current consumption and future consumption, while future goods are only able to provide for future consumption.</li><li>(Böhm-Bawerk) People have a lack of will, an imaginative mind, or are uncertain of the future, and thus would prefer to consume today than in the future.</li><li>(Böhm-Bawerk) Receiving a consumption good today means freeing up resources for investment in capital goods, which provides long term benefit. Receiving a consumption good in the future does not allow this.</li><li>(Productivity theory) </li></ol><h4>References</h4>","Political leaders should not be able to profit from their offices":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Political leaders should not be able to profit from their office for a variety of reasons.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is something that Aristotle makes very clear.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are several explanation for the prevention of political leaders from taking advantage of their positions:</p><ul><li>It means the people running of office are not people who are greedy.</li><li>It increases trust in the government.</li><li>It shifts the goals of the people in power to actually running the state well, rather than ruling for personal benefit.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The profit motive sharpens incentives\">The profit motive sharpens incentives</a><ul><li>This means that political leaders would rule in a way that benefits them, not the wider society.</li></ul></li></ul>","Politics is the fullest realization of prudence, and it is divided into the legislative and political sciences":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Politics is the virtue of prudence when applied to ruling a state.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Politics is what Aristotle considers to be the most important of the activities that an individual ought to engage in.</p><h4>Legislative vs. Political</h4><ul><li><strong>Legislative Science:</strong> deals with the general outlines of running a state well, i.e., writing laws, etc.</li><li><strong>Political Science:</strong> deals with the particulars that arise when running a state, and thus engaging in the action (last step in the deliberative process). </li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Rising populations in the mid-1700s led to social discontent":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the increasing prevalent of the potato as a food, and the ease with which it can be grown, population boomed beginning during the mid-1700s, leading to social discontent.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This social discontent coincided with Enlightenment ideas, and thus produced solutions and reform movements.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>This had many effects during the century:<ul><li>Aristocrats could not find posts for all of their sons (leading to their discontent)</li><li>Peasants couldn’t leave their land to all their children (creating a class of landless peasants)</li><li>This surfeit of population would fuel the armies during the French Revolutionary Wars.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Small neglected problems compound into the shape of a whole life":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A failure that would have been trivial to correct at the time can, left alone, come to stand for a pattern of avoidance that determines everything after it, so the scale of a neglect is no guide to its consequences.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the structure that makes regret intelligible, since what is regretted is rarely a large decision and usually a small omission that turned out to be load-bearing.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Hemingway builds <em>The Snows of Kilimanjaro</em> on the figure: Harry’s gangrene begins as a scratch he does not treat, and it stands for the talent he traded away in exchange for comfort, one small accommodation at a time.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Distractions prevent us from fulfilling our life goals\">Distractions prevent us from fulfilling our life goals</a><ul><li>The mechanism by which the accommodations accumulate.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In life, the little things often matter more than the big moments\">In life, the little things often matter more than the big moments</a><ul><li>The same claim in its positive form.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In life, one has nothing to regret\">In life, one has nothing to regret</a><ul><li>The opposing counsel.</li></ul></li></ul>","Suggestiveness, not articulateness, is the ideal of Chinese art":"<h4>Summary</h4><h4>Significance</h4><p>Suggestiveness is the fundamental feature of Chinese art, and something that differentiates Chinese art with Western art.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>This is a pattern that can be seen through various mediums and definitions of Chinese art:</p><ul><li>Most obviously, it manifests in paintings and other artworks of a visual nature.</li><li>It also manifests in Chinese literature, which is often phrased more ambiguously than comparative Western ones.</li><li>Specifically, it affects Chinese philosophy, whose writings are often very ambiguous and pithy.</li></ul><p>Fong Youlan suggests that this is because Chinese philosophy strives to communicate the maximum possible with the most minimal medium.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Zhuangzi</em></p><p>A fish-trap is for catching fish; once you've caught the fish, you can forget about the trap. A rabbit-snare is for catching rabbits; once you've caught the rabbit, you can forget about the snare. <strong>Words are for catching ideas; once you've caught the idea, you can forget about the words.</strong></p></blockquote><p>Additionally, the central purpose of Chinese philosophy is to seek the <em>dao</em>, though it is said that the <em>dao</em> cannot be told, but only suggested.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"荷花图轴\">荷花图轴</a><ul><li>This is an example of visual piece of Chinese art that displays the characteristics described herein.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Eels\">Eels</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Assumption and Coronation of the Virgin":"<h4>Notes</h4><p>Excellent composition and use of color. Also the way that the expressions of the figures are rendered in an especially compelling way.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Mater Dolorosa\">Mater Dolorosa</a><ul><li>Addresses the same subject, though with vastly different tones.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Romans switched to a comprehensive defense strategy as centralized states developed along its borders":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>With the development of powerful tribal confederations along the Northern border, the arrival of central Asian nomadic groups, and powerful Eastern states, Rome had to utilize a comprehensive defensive strategy rather than the client state system as they had done before.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the evolution of Rome’s defense system with the passing of time, as the prior system of client systems stopped working with the development of centralized states along Rome’s borders.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Following the Crisis of the Third Century, the Roman military became centralized around the emperor\">Following the Crisis of the Third Century, the Roman military became centralized around the emperor</a><ul><li>This explains the final evolution of Roman border defense, as the military became concentrated around the emperor, rather than being spread across the frontier.</li></ul></li></ul>","The benefactor typically loves his beneficiary more than the other way around":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle argues that benefactors love their beneficiary more than the other way around.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is used to explain why parents love their children more than the other way around, and lay down a rule about the relationship between benefactor and beneficiary.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is because the maker typically loves his work more than his work loves him, since bringing into existence is a choice.</p><ul><li>Additionally, a maker of something is defined by the thing that he makes. I.e., we exist through our activity.</li><li>Craftsman and poets love their creation. In the same way, the benefactor loves the beneficiary.</li><li>Giving is noble, whereas receiving is merely advantageous.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The blind are able to see what the sighted are not":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Being able to “see” something could often times mean more than simply being able to observe something concrete. It can often be the case that in works of literature, those who are blind are able to see the “truth” in a more direct manner than those who can physically see.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a type of symbolism that is common in many works of art and literature, especially of the blind prophet who can see much clearer than some other figure of authority who is able to see.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">How terrible—to see the truth when the truth is only pain to him who sees!</span><ul><li>The blind seer is the only one that is able to see the truth.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Let me tell you this.. You with your precious eyes, you're blind to the corruption of your life, to the house you live in, those you live with— who are your parents? Do you know?</span><ul><li>Although Oedipus is able to see, he is blind to the truth, the life that he actually lives.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">OEDIPUS: Riddles—all you can say are riddles, murk and darkness. TIRESIAS: Ah, but aren't you the best man alive at solving riddles?</span><ul><li>Not only is this line really funny, it again connects back to the idea that although Oedipus is able to see, he is unable to see the truth, only able to see “riddles, murk, and darkness.”</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Go in and reflect on that, solve that. And if you find I've lied from this day onward call the prophet blind.</span><ul><li>While again, the prophet mocks Oedipus’ ability to solve riddles, he again states his ability to see the truth, despite being physically blind.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">A prophet? Well then, free yourself of every charge!</span><ul><li>While Jocasta seems to disbelieve the nature of prophecies, after saying this, she tells a prophecy that is where Oedipus is first beginning to actually be concern about who he is.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">I have a terrible fear the blind seer can see.</span><ul><li>This is the moment when Oedipus realizes Tiresias’ power.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Brave words, and you'd persuade me if mother weren't alive. But mother lives, so for all your reassurances I live in fear, I must.</span><ul><li>It seems that Oedipus has entirely been convinced by the power of the prophecies, even after Polybus’ death, which seemed to invalidate the premise of the prophecy according to Jocasta.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">You, you'll see no more the pain I suffered, all the pain I caused! Too,long you looked on the ones you never should have seen, blind to the ones you longed to see, to know! Blind from this hour on! Blind in the darkness—blind!</span><ul><li>The moment Oedipus realizes the truth, he blinds himself. </li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Who will marry you then? Not a man on earth. Your doom is clear: you'll wither away to nothing, single, without a child.</span><ul><li>Here, after Oedipus has been blinded, he is able to correctly predict the future for his children, at least Antigone.</li></ul></li><li>During the entirety of the play when Oedipus was sighted, he was entirely unable to even see the true nature of his family. When he finds about the truth coincides with then he blinds himself. </li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our fate is determined from birth\">Our fate is determined from birth</a><ul><li>The blind are usually able to see true fate.</li></ul></li></ul>","The invisible hand was advanced narrowly and with caveats":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The metaphor appears once in the founding text of the discipline, is applied to a narrow case, and is hedged more heavily than its author’s usual practice, which makes the modern doctrine attributed to it an extrapolation rather than an inheritance.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>A large part of the case for laissez-faire is conducted by appeal to authority, and the authority turns out not to have held the position in the strong form in which it is cited.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Smith’s own worry was that if merchants pursue their interest by seeking regulation and privilege rather than by producing, the mechanism does not operate at all. Rothschild goes further and reads the metaphor as ironic, which is speculative; the narrow claim about frequency, scope and hedging does not depend on that reading.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Threats to economic freedom come from guilds, corporations and local bodies as much as from the central state\">Threats to economic freedom come from guilds, corporations and local bodies as much as from the central state</a><ul><li>The worry that the caveats express.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The idea that self-interest can produce overall benefits is an inherently consequentialist one\">The idea that self-interest can produce overall benefits is an inherently consequentialist one</a><ul><li>What the doctrine amounts to once stated carefully.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Encompassing interests mean that an actor&#x27;s private optimum resembles the social optimum; narrow interests mean that an actor&#x27;s gains most from rent-seeking\">Encompassing interests mean that an actor's private optimum resembles the social optimum; narrow interests mean that an actor's gains most from rent-seeking</a><ul><li>The modern statement of the condition under which the mechanism works.</li></ul></li></ul>","The overwhelming power of labor unions in the post-war consensus harmed the UK economy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>As the environment of the post-war consensus as highly conducive to the development of labor unions, as labor unions grew more numerous and larger, they began to have a chokehold over the economy and slow down its productivity growth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why the UK shifted to such radical neoliberalism in the 1980s, since this socialist approach to management simple had not produced results.</p><h4>Effect of Labor Unions</h4><p>There was very little productivity growth compared to the other European nations and low levels of investment in the economy. The UK became termed the “Sick Man of Europe,” This reached a nadir from 1973-1979, when a combination of supply-side shocks, inflation, and labor union strikes wiped out British economics growth. During the Winter of Discontent (late 1978-early 1979), the situation was at its worse.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Mark Kesselman, <em>Introduction to Comparative Politics</em></p><p>It seemed as if everyone was on strike. Strikes by truckers disrupted fuel supplies. Strikes by train drivers disrupted intercity commerce and visits to Granny. Some ambulance drivers refused to respond to emergency calls. Gravediggers refused to bury the dead.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4>","The second wave of democratization (1945-1962) ended with 1960s authoritarian reversals":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The second wave of democracy began around 1945 and ended around the early 1960s.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This period saw many major democracies established as a result of American efforts to rebuild the West, and British decolonization.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The second wave of democracy began after the end of WWII, when much of Western Europe was re-democratized and decolonization began, leading to the formation of new democracies. This came to halt by the 1960s, as democratic norms began to dissolve, partly due to the rivalry created by the Cold War leading to both sides prioritizing strategic alliances over democratic ideals.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The first wave of democratization (1820s-1922) ended with Mussolini&#x27;s Rise\">The first wave of democratization (1820s-1922) ended with Mussolini's Rise</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR\">The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR</a></li></ul>","To achieve anything, one must begin with the opposite":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>To be able to achieve anything and preserve that thing, we must acknowledge and understand the opposite of that thing and always keep some aspect of the opposite in the thing that we are trying to accomplish.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the fundamental principle by which Taoism, according to Laozi, operates.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>For example, if one wants to be strong, then one must start with a feeling of one being weak, and once we become strong, we must continue to understand what made us weak in the first place and counteract that. Another manifestation of this principle comes in way that arrogance manifests, as Laozi states that arrogance is the sign that one’s advancement has reached its extreme limit, since in arrogance one has forgot the opposite of what one was attempting to achieve.</p><ul><li>Humility is therefore the sign that one’s limit is far from reached.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Trade civilizes people and encourages lower time preference, cooperation, and predictability":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that trade makes people more civilized, self-controlled (lower time preference), more willing to cooperate, and more predictable. People rely more on trade and bargaining and contracts than violence.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents the Doux Commerce theory, which has been highly influential.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Studies have shown that trade flows increase the cost of conflict, which has the effect of reducing the level of conflict. Additionally, through looking at the initial days of wide-body aircraft which allowed for more goods to be transported, researches found that more trade resulted in a significant decrease in conflict. All of this evidence points to the fact that trade does seem to mitigate conflict.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As societies become more modern and wealthy, they shift towards liberal democracy\">As societies become more modern and wealthy, they shift towards liberal democracy</a><ul><li>This is related.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation\">Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation</a><ul><li>The opposite idea.</li></ul></li></ul>","When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure, since the original phenomenon that the measure was intending to record may be distorted by attempts to increase the observed measurement.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a general adage that applies to many areas of study, but especially when it comes to economics, since the measurements are the only way to determine the success of a policy. Furthermore, since all systems are, at their core, meant to be optimizers, then it seems that the best heuristic when it comes to choosing between systems is choosing the system which minimizes loss/overfitting.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This can fundamentally be explained by the fact that all optimization is subject to some amount of overfitting. This means that while a system seems to have been trained in order to optimize for some goal, this goal for which it is optimizing is actually not representative of the overall nature of the the goal at hand. Over a long enough time scale, the optimization will show its cracks and will no longer track the original intention of the optimization.</p><ul><li>Practically, this seems to be the result of informational frictions, which arise through the conversion of information from one form to another, or the failure for information to be expressed in a succinct yet accurate and precise way.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trust measurement for the quality of institutions\">Trust measurement for the quality of institutions</a><ul><li>A potential measurement that could become a bad measure, if it is pursued incessantly?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"AI alignment suffers from two interconnected problems of overfitting\">AI alignment suffers from two interconnected problems of overfitting</a><ul><li>I.e., how well the mesa objective of the AI matches with the overall goal, and how well the AI is able to optimize for the mesa goal.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should pursue autonomous AI governance rather than AI containment\">We should pursue autonomous AI governance rather than AI containment</a><ul><li>Perhaps this strategy allows for a smaller loss function/overfitting?</li></ul></li></ul>","荷花图轴":"<h4>Notes</h4><h4>References</h4>"});
